A||||5||ezsurf||Cylinder Compression
Z||000000||ezsurf||04-19-99||08:28 PM||||<br>I have just purchased a 1986 Fiero SE.  I am considering rebuilding the engine.  But, I want to do a compression check first to see if it needs it.  I have the Pontiac service manual for this car (Helm publications).  The book says the compressiojn ratio is 8.5:1/8.9:1. I take this to mean that the compression ratio is from no less than 8.5:1 to no more than 8.9:1. It also says the compression of the lowest cylinder should be no less than 70% of the highest cylinder, and no cylinder should be less than 100psi. However, it does not say what the cylinder compression is supposed to be.  No Pontiac dealer has been able to tell me what the compression is supposed to be. There must be a book somewhere, or a conversion formula to convert compression ratio to pounds of compression.  Does anyone have a clue?
Z||000001||theogre||04-19-99||09:54 PM||||think about it a minute....... if one atmosphere of presure is about 14PSI then what's 8.x times that?<p>the helms book tell you the absolute minumum as well. 100 PSI. "The compression of the lowest cylinder should be no less than 70% of the highest cylinder, and no cylinder should be less than 100psi."<p>so if 1 ATM=14 PSI then 8.5ATM=119psi<br>tho I thought compression was closer to 9 on most 4 cyls. so lets say 8.9x14psi=124psi..... 70% of that is 87.2 PSI, and  a dead cyl according to the haynes book. Frankly after dealing with Haynes via e-mail, I'm not impressed with them or their books. I would not at all be suprised to find 70% is completely wrong.<p>I've always been taught you want no more than a 10-15 PSI differance top to bottom in compression. Other wise you get a really rough running motor. doing the math..... that's a low of 80+% of the high cyl.
Z||000002||ezsurf||04-20-99||05:40 PM||||Thanks for the info Ogre.  Your information makes more sense than anything I've heard so far.  The manual I'm using (Helm) is the original Pontiac dealer service manual.  It is not a Haynes manual.  The Helm manual will set you back about $80.  Atmospheric pressure 14.696 psi x 8.5:1/8.9:1 yeilds a cylinder pressure of 125 psi to 131 psi.  You are correct about the 4 cylinder engine, the compression ratio is 9.0:1, which is the spec listed in the book.  I now know what target I'm shooting for, thanks again. - ezsurf
Z||000003||theogre||04-20-99||07:56 PM||||no problem, sometimes the obvious things are most annoying.
Z||000004||ezsurf||04-24-99||02:11 PM||||Ogre,<br>I thought I had this compression thing fugured out.  I did the compression test today.  The lowest cylinder was 145psi.  All of the other cylinders were between 150 and 155psi.  I'm glad to see the compression looks good, but I still don't know how to figure pounds of compression from the compression ratio.  I'll keep looking, if you find anything let me know.
Z||000005||theogre||04-24-99||03:22 PM||||Hmmmmmmm..... That is a bit higher than then math I did. Not sure why. something dumb probably.<p>that range is a good motor tho. I definatly wouldn't worry about a 10psi top to bottom dif. Not for a street car anyways.
